{:edb/db-before-t 2347 :edb/db-after-t 2348 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535660 50 #inst "2026-10-09T04:36:36.190Z" 13194139535660 true] [17592186105576 1100 "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/9e8f3f1bb50432add6ba6c1b91fb5e1f7ab052e48ab155c2e92b40bc309e81a5.png)" 13194139535660 true] [17592186105576 1100 "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{2}{5},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5},\\end{vmatrix}x = 1; \\frac{4}{5}, \\mid x = 2g(x) = {\\frac{2}{5},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{10},\\end{vmatrix}x = 1h(x) = {\\frac{3}{10},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2},\\end{vmatrix}x = 1; \\frac{1}{5}, \\mid x = 2$" 13194139535660 false] [17592186105576 1145 "For a function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/9363d37d53374519f1170d2c1c24ecb690a6810a304207ef21a0d1f3eba609de.png) to be a valid probability mass function on a set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/5c3d9b70452a08b50ba5b826f5694c3d85c999754e5304ff71d47362a66534cd.png) it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/53ad6d309b94b7889b85ecc5d90ae0201d2ba7b25b2b82cc9b6c95d1165ba268.png) for all ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/86d18b1a03e111c64a7328566ad27a5ae12870cf2ee67e9750f5747bee71eb86.png) in ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/d4aaadce376b13dc707757d84b05abf9c95d436aa2e4bea8fb19a078eff136c6.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/024a7717c9fccd8dceb278a0a50934b514706a425b43f76ee9d229b9008b822b.png)\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/06a568b15033fbfbfc33c59b4f4c1b9382df53efb53025fa9bdf5714972c7440.png) is *not* a valid probability distribution because the value ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/d14540db55ffd098075a55e4c744e9cde80545517df1456959c8baea7500586e.png) does not satisfy the first condition (it is not between ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/637d7cec1da56f96138c07b108310c3d842ddc13b466234f78b4ee1757882a1b.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/5d9e7c41ca12f816fdb84db36a35ba66280982891484cbee9db4a7222c863d92.png)).\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/3d7612998c8458ff973fe19d9fa000175d6ab8ce6d2689d593852f0311e73b6b.png) is *not* a valid probability distribution because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/49f6627729021cf5e3e78f4dcbedb957495a385bccdb4484e5a5ac6f892b0161.png) do not sum to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/19ef2d374cfb64bc575f920e7413112e512fdda510e0fa71dfbd8297cf05d96d.png)\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/cb3ebaf420b7fc49cf896463ad37b95855da99cc6356e4795dc81df2d76f5503.png)\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/70bff1a95629c275ea93a10e6130b14e6565bb70465a778e1bff38526ad389da.png) is a valid probability distribution. Indeed, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/5392675a2894798e20c813a2bc0611940d9f37373f85ed4e474016d673d9a5e3.png)\nand\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/10c830df4267667cf3475f1a41b45d5ec27949c1395b00b7502325481dc93830.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069179/assets/3d8cbf1521bc8f20e8ad2dff7bf427089ee3e1e591cdba51102049daab5d70f5.png) only.\"" 13194139535660 true] [17592186105576 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function on a set $S$, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because the value $f(1)=-\\dfrac15$ does not satisfy the first condition (it is not between $0$ and $1$).\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of $h$ do not sum to $1{:}$\n$\\dfrac25 + \\dfrac{3}{10} = \\dfrac{7}{10} \\neq 1$\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability distribution. Indeed, we have\n$0 \\leq \\dfrac{3}{10}, \\dfrac12, \\dfrac15 \\leq 1$\nand\n$\\dfrac{3}{10} + \\dfrac12 + \\dfrac15 = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\"" 13194139535660 false] [17592186105577 1100 "Compute $P\\left(X \\leq 3 \\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X=x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ |" 13194139535660 false] [17592186105577 1100 "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X=x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ |\n\nCompute $P\\left( X \\leq 3 \\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above." 13194139535660 true] [17592186105577 1145 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3) &= P(x ∈ {1, 2, 3}) \\\\\n&= P(X = 1) + P(X = 2) + P(X = 3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{3}{10} + \\frac{1}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535660 false] [17592186105577 1145 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(x\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535660 true] [17592186105578 1100 "Compute $P\\left(X\\in \\{2,4,6\\}\\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table above.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |" 13194139535660 false] [17592186105578 1100 "| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P\\left(X\\in \\{2,4,6\\}\\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table above." 13194139535660 true] [17592186105578 1145 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X ∈ {2, 4, 6}) &= P(X = 2) + P(X = 4) + P(X = 6) \\\\\n&= f(2) + f(4) + f(6) \\\\\n&= 0.1 + 0.1 + 0.2 \\\\\n&= 0.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535660 false] [17592186105578 1145 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\in {2,4,6}) & =P(X=2)+P(X=4)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(4)+f(6) \\\\  & =0.1+0.1+0.2 \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535660 true] [17592186105738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359708 13194139535660 true] [17592186105738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359722 13194139535660 true] [17592186105739 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359685 13194139535660 true] [17592186105740 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359692 13194139535660 true] [17592186105740 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359699 13194139535660 true] [17592186105741 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359715 13194139535660 true] [17592186106428 1017 #edb/ref 17592186105077 13194139535660 false] [17592186106428 1017 #edb/ref 17592186359702 13194139535660 true] [17592186106428 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535660 true] [17592186106428 1100 "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}, & x=3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}, & x=7\\end{aligned}\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}, & x=3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}, & x=7\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}, & x=3 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}, & x=7\\end{aligned}\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535660 true] [17592186106428 1100 "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{1}{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3},\\end{vmatrix}x = 7g(x) = {\\frac{1}{3},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3},\\end{vmatrix}x = 7h(x) = {\\frac{3}{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 3 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2},\\end{vmatrix}x = 7$" 13194139535660 false] [17592186106428 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0\\le f(x)\\le 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{x\\in S}f(x)=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability mass function because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of $f$ do not sum to $1:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{7}{6}\\ne 1\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is a valid probability mass function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n0\\le \\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}\\le 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=1.\n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because the value $h(3)=\\frac{3}{2}$ does not satisfy the first condition (it is not between $0$ and $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ only.\"" 13194139535660 true] [17592186106429 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535660 true] [17592186106429 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X = x)$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{4}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n\nCompute $P(X ∈ {\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{3}{2}})$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above." 13194139535660 false] [17592186106429 1100 "| $x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X=x)$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{4}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n\nCompute $P(X\\in {\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}})$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above." 13194139535660 true] [17592186106429 1145 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\in {\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}}) & =P(X=\\frac{1}{2})+P(X=\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{4}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535660 true] [17592186359685 1017 #edb/ref 17592186359686 13194139535660 true] [17592186359685 1089 "q-124711" 13194139535660 true] [17592186359685 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535660 true] [17592186359685 1100 "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}, & x=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}, & x=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}, & x=\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{25}, & x=0 \\\\ \\frac{23}{25}, & x=1\\end{aligned}\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535660 true] [17592186359685 1113 #uuid "fbba20f1-3fd6-53dd-bbd5-acc5b1346e12" 13194139535660 true] [17592186359685 1145 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0\\le f(x)\\le 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{x\\in S}f(x)=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of $f$ do not sum to $1:$\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{1}{5}\\ne 1\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is a valid probability mass function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n0\\le \\frac{1}{5},\\frac{4}{5}\\le 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}=1.\n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability mass function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n0\\le \\frac{2}{25},\\frac{23}{25}\\le 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{25}+\\frac{23}{25}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" 13194139535660 true] [17592186359686 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359687 13194139535660 true] [17592186359686 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359688 13194139535660 true] [17592186359686 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359689 13194139535660 true] [17592186359686 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359690 13194139535660 true] [17592186359686 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359691 13194139535660 true] [17592186359686 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535660 true] [17592186359686 1041 "selection" 13194139535660 true] [17592186359686 1104 #edb/ref 17592186359691 13194139535660 true] [17592186359686 1110 #uuid "977c071a-217a-56f5-afaa-79ec0b195d60" 13194139535660 true] [17592186359687 1034 "$f$ and $g$ only" 13194139535660 true] [17592186359687 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535660 true] [17592186359687 1111 #uuid "10c25dc7-2ab9-5a90-a94d-53f0652659af" 13194139535660 true] [17592186359688 1034 "$h$ only" 13194139535660 true] [17592186359688 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535660 true] [17592186359688 1111 #uuid "f28b16a2-7c1b-5f04-a88b-1537c4b3bc28" 13194139535660 true] [17592186359689 1034 "$f$, $g$ and $h$" 13194139535660 true] [17592186359689 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535660 true] [17592186359689 1111 #uuid "7979cde7-f8ca-513e-8abf-26f5ab52c463" 13194139535660 true] [17592186359690 1034 "$g$ only" 13194139535660 true] [